1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,950
Raja Ravi Varma painted the Indian epics in oil.

2
00:00:04,000 --> 00:00:07,450
What if he could paint any scene you describe?

3
00:00:07,500 --> 00:00:11,950
This is The Next Ravi Varma, by Team The Unsupervised.

4
00:00:12,000 --> 00:00:14,950
You type a scene from the Mahabharata or Ramayana,

5
00:00:15,000 --> 00:00:17,950
optionally add a photo of someone posing,

6
00:00:18,000 --> 00:00:21,950
and get a new painting in his oil style.

7
00:00:22,000 --> 00:00:25,550
Our project follows the four milestones of our proposal:

8
00:00:25,600 --> 00:00:31,950
the data, the style, the words, and the pose, all inside one web app.

9
00:00:32,000 --> 00:00:35,950
Milestone one: the data. From 135 images,

10
00:00:36,000 --> 00:00:44,950
we kept only his 61 oil paintings, not the flat printed copies, and held nine out for testing.

11
00:00:45,000 --> 00:00:48,950
Qwen3-VL captioned every painting,

12
00:00:49,000 --> 00:00:53,950
and each caption starts with our trigger phrase: rrvarma oil painting.

13
00:00:54,000 --> 00:01:00,950
Milestone two: the style. We trained a three-megabyte LoRA on frozen Stable Diffusion 1.5.

14
00:01:01,000 --> 00:01:06,950
Plain Stable Diffusion paints a photo, and asking for Ravi Varma gives a calendar print.

15
00:01:07,000 --> 00:01:15,950
Our LoRA paints in oil: style similarity 0.82, close to 0.84 for his real paintings.

16
00:01:16,000 --> 00:01:21,950
Milestone three: the words. Qwen 2.5 expands a scene into a full prompt.

17
00:01:22,000 --> 00:01:25,950
A CLIP judge then picks the best of four paintings,

18
00:01:26,000 --> 00:01:29,950
raising scene match from 28 to 30.

19
00:01:30,000 --> 00:01:33,950
Milestone four: the pose. OpenPose turns a photo into a skeleton,

20
00:01:34,000 --> 00:01:38,450
and ControlNet paints a figure that follows it,

21
00:01:38,500 --> 00:01:43,950
with character looks, pose checks and a high-resolution refine.

22
00:01:44,000 --> 00:01:47,950
Text alone gets poses wrong and adds extra people.

23
00:01:48,000 --> 00:01:51,950
With a pose photo, pose accuracy jumps from 10 to 97 percent,

24
00:01:52,000 --> 00:01:55,950
with no loss in scene match.

25
00:01:56,000 --> 00:02:01,950
Here is our web app. I type a scene and press Paint.

26
00:02:02,000 --> 00:02:08,950
Four paintings are made and the best is kept; the prompt and seed are shown below.

27
00:02:09,000 --> 00:02:17,950
Fast mode gives a quick single-image preview.

28
00:02:18,000 --> 00:02:24,950
Now I upload a photo of someone drawing a bow and press Paint.

29
00:02:25,000 --> 00:02:29,950
The app shows the skeleton and paints Chitrangada in exactly that pose.

30
00:02:30,000 --> 00:02:33,950
All of this runs on a free Colab T4 GPU.

31
00:02:34,000 --> 00:02:38,950
His style, any scene, any pose.

32
00:02:39,000 --> 00:02:45,950
Our code, data and evaluations are public on GitHub. Thank you.

